  "query_preview": "..\n(Saturday through Tuesday)\nIssued at 1113 AM CDT Wed Sep 30 2026\n\nTo our west, an upper level trough will begin its swing through \nthe TN Valley over the weekend. At the surface, an accompanying \ncold front will be pushing east. Weak WAA and strong low level\nmoisture advection will be present along and ahead of this \nsystem. As such, as we move into Saturday morning we will have an\nincredibly moist environment present, priming us for a weekend of\nheavy rainfall and possible flash flooding concerns.\n\nThrough the weekend we will see dew points in the 70s and PWATs \nabove the 90th percentile per BMX sounding climatology (2\"+). This\nenvironment will be favorable for efficient rainfall production. \nWith persistent forcing from Friday night through Sunday \nafternoon, high rain chances (60-80%) are in the forecast all \nweekend. Models indicate 2-4 inches of rain from Friday through \nMonday. Flooding concerns will arise if periods of heavy rainfall \ncause significant amounts of rain to fall in a short period of \ntime. As such, WPC has issued a Slight Risk (level 2/4) for \nexcessive rainfall for portions of the area on Saturday into \nSunday. Some uncertainty remains regarding the QPF totals due to \nthe fact the front and parent trough will be weakening as they \nmove through. Should the system come through weaker than currently\nforecast, QPF amounts could trend down.\n\nThe front is currently forecast to pass during the day on Sunday, \nhowever low rain chances and cloud cover look to linger behind the \nfront through Tuesday. The lingering cloud cover paired with the slow\ninfiltration of cooler and drier air from the north will keep highs\nin the 70s and lows in the 50s for the start of the work week.",
